{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And the flesh.</b> Of sacred shelamim offerings — <b>that touches any unclean thing shall not be eaten.</b>" "<b>And the flesh.</b> To include a limb part of which went outside — that the inner part is permitted (Sifra)." "<b>Whoever is clean may eat the flesh.</b> What is this saying? Since it is said ״וְדַם זְבָחֶיךָ יִשָּׁפֵךְ״ and so forth, ״וְהַבָּשָׂר תֹּאכֵל״ (Deuteronomy 12), one might think they may not eat shelamim except the owners — therefore it says ״כָּל טָהוֹר יֹאכַל בָּשָׂר״ (Sifra)." "(<b>And the flesh — whoever is clean may eat the flesh.</b> That is to say: all that I forbade to you in the sin-offering and the guilt-offering, which if they went outside the curtains are forbidden — as it is written ״בְּחַצַר אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד יֹאכְלוּהָ״ (Leviticus 6) — regarding this flesh I say to you: ״כָּל טָהוֹר יֹאכַל בָּשָׂר״, even in the entire city.)"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:05:24.734974877Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}